Context (pp. 42–44 of the 'variations sur l'équation des lacets'): Grothendieck wants a purely group-theoretic, 'loop-group' version of the classical passage between genus 1 and genus 0 given by the elliptic involution x ↦ −x.

Starting data. π' is a loop group ('groupe à lacets') of type (1,1). Its topological model is π_1(E∖{0}) for an elliptic curve or real torus E. On the previous page he notes that π' carries a canonical outer action of G = Z/2Z, the involution −1, which gives an extension (58) 1→π'→𝓔→Z/2→1. In the model 𝓔 is the orbifold fundamental group of (E∖{0})/±1. That orbifold is a sphere with three cone points of order 2 and one puncture. Hence there are exactly three π'-conjugacy classes of splittings, matching the three nonzero 2-torsion points of E.

The 'fourgons de lacets simultanés'. This is an announced, not yet written (partly conjectural) theory of loop structures compatible with a finite group of outer operators. It is supposed to construct canonically the following:
(i) an outer group π̃' of type Π_{1,4}, modelled on π_1(E∖₂E), where ₂E is the 2-torsion subgroup;
(ii) an outer action of G on π̃';
(iii) a distinguished cusp i_0 ∈ I(π̃'), with |I| = 4, and J = I∖{i_0} of cardinality 3;
(iv) an isomorphism (59) π̃'(J) ≅ π', compatible with G. Here π̃'(J) is obtained by 'filling the holes at J'; it is of type (1,1), the model being E∖{0} ⊃ E∖₂E.
A marginal correction states that G in fact acts trivially on I(π̃'). Indeed each 2-torsion point is fixed by −1.

The quotient. Since −x = x iff 2x = 0, G acts freely on E∖₂E. So the extension (60) 1→π̃'→ξ̃→Z/2→1 is the mixed fundamental group of (E∖₂E, G). It equals the ordinary π_1 of V = (E∖₂E)/±1 (next page, (61)), which is P^1 minus 4 points, type (0,4). Thus ξ̃ (written Σ̃ on the next page) becomes a loop group of type (0,4). Its cusps satisfy I(Σ̃) = I(π̃')/G. Filling the cusp i_0 (the image of 0) yields a loop group π = 𝓛(π') of type (0,3), in (63). The next page then starts the inverse passage (0,3) → (1,1).

In modern terms: this is a combinatorial/anabelian version of the Legendre / 2-torsion construction relating M_{1,1} and M_{0,4} (λ-line), and Π_{1,1} with Π_{0,4}, Π_{0,3}. The purpose is to transport Galois/GT actions, and the 'équation des lacets', between genus 1 and genus 0.
